 Resuming starts a fresh job while preserving the original checkpoint until all chapters render successfully. Checkpoint writes and syncs are best effort, so an interrupted resumed job can leave both the original and new checkpoints available; either can reuse the shared chapter cache. Closing an unstarted response or interrupting the render leaves the original plan available, including when a replacement checkpoint could not be saved. A render with no failed chapters retires the original even if the replacement checkpoint could not be saved. A partial output keeps the original plan available to retry its failed chapters.
 
 Cache pruning runs when a render exits, including after Stop, and evicts unused chapter and segment audio oldest first. Each render reserves existing inputs before reporting progress or loading its engine, then keeps the whole plan's chapter and segment inputs protected until it exits. This includes later chapters and reusable segments that a resumed book has not reached yet, plus temporary WAVs being published. Unrelated audio becomes evictable once the plan's inputs are identified. Protected files and JSON bookkeeping needed to locate older entries after moving the data directory count toward the cache size, so the budget may be exceeded until a later render can evict them. An unsuccessful or cancelled FFmpeg assembly removes its incomplete output and retains the recovery checkpoint.
